THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS REDISTRIBUTED INSIDE THE INSTALLER
========================================================

The CamBridge-Setup.exe installer carries the following components. They are
separate programs bundled with, not linked into, CamBridge.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. ffmpeg
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Download : https://www.gyan.dev/ffmpeg/builds/
  Build    : gyan.dev ffmpeg-release-essentials
  License  : GPL-2.0-or-later (ffmpeg itself); the build enables GPL components

  A complete, unmodified copy of ffmpeg.exe is redistributed in the installer.
  Its license text ships alongside it as ffmpeg\LICENSE.txt.

  ffmpeg is executed as a separate process; no ffmpeg code is linked into
  CamBridge. CamBridge is itself GPL-3.0, so the combined distribution is
  compatible either way.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. LocosLab Virtual Camera
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Project  : https://github.com/locosmac/virtual-camera
  Component: LocosLab.MediaSource.dll  (virtual-camera 1.0.0)
  License  : MIT

  A Media Foundation custom media source built on the MFCreateVirtualCamera
  API. It is installed by an MSI to
  "C:\Program Files\LocosLab GmbH\LocosLab Virtual Camera" and registered as
  an in-process COM server; it is the piece that lets a user-mode application
  publish frames as a real camera device.

  The license text ships with the installer as LICENSE-locoslab.rtf.

  Modification notice: LocosLab Virtual Camera is used unmodified. CamBridge
  supplies frames to it through a named pipe using its public .NET API
  (LocosLab.VirtualCamera). The frame generator that reads from that pipe is
  the only CamBridge-specific part, and it lives in src\BridgeCore.cs.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Windows Media Foundation runtime (not redistributed)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The Media Foundation virtual camera API (MFCreateVirtualCamera) is part of
  Windows itself and is used at run time. No Microsoft binaries are shipped.
